    "number": 81,
    "id": "630680736622",
    "english_prompt": "Yes\nFrom the options select the correct Gujarati translation of the English sentence given below.\nMahesh came to the school yesterday.\nગઈકાલે મહેશ શાળાઅે અાવ્યાે.\nગઈકાલે મહેશ શાળાઅે અાવ્યાે હતાે.\nગઈકાલે મહેશ જ શાળાઅે અાવ્યાે હતાે.\nમહેશ ગઈકાલે શાળાઅે અાવે છે.\nSub-Section Number : 2\nSub-Section Id : 63068044228\nQuestion Shuffling Allowed : Yes\nQuestion Id : 630680736595 Question Type : COMPREHENSION Sub Question Shuffling\nAllowed : Yes Group Comprehension Questions : No Question Pattern Type : NonMatrix\nQuestion Numbers : (82 to 85)\nRead the following passage and answer the following questions.\nThe decline of reading in India is a concerning trend that reflects broader shifts in societal\nvalues, educational priorities, and technological advancements. While reading has long been\ncelebrated as a cornerstone of intellectual development and cultural enrichment, various\nfactors have contributed to its decline in recent years.\nOne significant factor contributing to the decline of reading in India is the rise of digital media\nand entertainment options. With the proliferation of smartphones, social media platforms,\nstreaming services, and online gaming, many individuals, particularly the younger generation,\nare increasingly turning to digital distractions for leisure and information consumption. The\nallure of quick and easily accessible entertainment through videos, memes, and short-form\ncontent has led to a decrease in the time allocated for traditional reading activities.\nAdditionally, changes in educational practices and curriculum emphasis may also play a role in\ndiminishing reading habits among students. The pressure to perform well in standardized\ntests and the focus on rote learning rather than critical thinking skills may result in reduced\nemphasis on reading for pleasure and intellectual curiosity. As a result, students may view\nreading as a chore rather than a source of enjoyment and personal growth.\nFurthermore, socio-economic factors such as income inequality, lack of access to quality\neducation, and limited availability of reading materials can exacerbate the decline of reading in\nIndia. Many individuals from marginalized communities may not have access to libraries,\nbookstores, or educational resources, making it challenging for them to cultivate a reading\nhabit. Moreover, the high cost of books and the prioritization of basic needs over discretionary\nspending on literature can further hinder widespread reading culture.\nThe decline of reading in India is not without consequences. Reading plays a crucial role in\nfostering critical thinking skills, empathy, cultural understanding, and language proficiency. It\nserves as a means of preserving cultural heritage, fostering imagination, and promoting\nlifelong learning. Therefore, efforts to reverse the decline of reading should involve a multi-\nfaceted approach, including initiatives to promote reading culture in schools, libraries, and\ncommunities, as well as measures to improve access to affordable and diverse reading\nmaterials.\nIn conclusion, while the decline of reading in India poses significant challenges, it also presents\nopportunities for intervention and revitalization. By addressing underlying socio-economic\nbarriers, reimagining educational practices, and harnessing the potential of digital\ntechnologies to promote reading, India can work towards fostering a vibrant reading culture\nthat enriches individual lives and strengthens societal cohesion.\nSub questions",
